# {app_name}

Tree-sitter parser for {languages_text}, packaged for [Lumis](https://lumis.sh).

## Installation

```elixir
def deps do
  [
    {:lumis, "~> 0.8"},
    {:{app_name}, "~> {hex_version}"}
  ]
end
```

Lumis loads only the parsers your project depends on, so adding this is what
lets it highlight {languages_text}. Nothing is downloaded at runtime.

## What it contains

`priv/parsers/` holds the compiled WASM parser and the manifest describing it —
its size, its SHA-256, and the queries that drive highlighting. Lumis reads them
from there, and `mix release` carries them like any dependency's assets.

No code: this application starts nothing and supervises nothing.

## Parser

| Upstream | Version | Revision |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [{git_url}]({git_url}) | {upstream_version} | {rev} |

Built with [`tree-sitter build --wasm`](https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/cli/build.html) v{tree_sitter_cli_version}.

The same parser is published to npm as
[`{npm_package}`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/{npm_package}) — byte for byte,
from one build, so both registries serve the same artifact.

